Derive inventory time from individual residence intervals (@engineering-production-inventory-time: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "Compare-production-designs-with-evidence-and-uncertainty-about-the-future.lingua#r_005D33EC2ADADFCFCC3E4CE8CD") { r_007AFDCF49CEB99954FFE85139
An inventory count at an instant and a residence time per item measure different things. Connect them by following identified items through a declared boundary. Let item i enter at a_i and leave at d_i≥a_i. Its presence indicator is one for a_i≤t<d_i and zero otherwise. For a finite observed collection, N(t) is the sum of these indicators. Include processing items as well as waiting items if the boundary contains both.

On observation window [u,v], the area under N is A=∫ᵤᵛN(t)dt. Integrating the finite sum gives A=Σ max(0,min(d_i,v)−max(a_i,u)). Each term is the length of the portion of an item's residence inside the window. This proves an exact identity with units item-time. Endpoints of intervals have zero duration, so a consistent half-open convention handles simultaneous entries/exits without changing the area. Reuse [the shared integral definition](../../mathematics/core/Define-the-integral-and-distinguish-net-change-from-total-magnitude.lingua#r_0AEC763D73DF3124CDBB2E86D1) and the earlier balance preparation rather than treating a graph's area as a new empirical law.

An original boundary example has intervals (−3,1),(0,2),(3,7) and window [0,5]. The clipped residence lengths are one, two and two, so A=5 and average WIP is one. The two items that depart in the window have full residence times four and two. Multiplying their departure rate 2/5 by their mean full residence three gives 6/5, not one. Initial inventory contributes time before the window and unfinished work contributes time inside it; an uncorrected completed-item average mixes these boundaries.

For an initially empty system, assume all arrivals are at or after zero, finitely many arrive in every bounded interval, and consider [0,T]. Separate completed departures from unfinished items. Then A(T)=Σ_completed(d_i−a_i)+R(T), where R(T)=Σ_unfinished(T−a_i). If D(T)>0 completed items departed, define W_D(T) as their mean full residence. Exactly A(T)/T=[D(T)/T]W_D(T)+R(T)/T. If the system is also empty at T, R=0 and the finite identity is exact without randomness or a steady-state assumption. If D=0, do not define its mean by dividing by zero; use the area equation directly.

[Manufacturing foundations and the inventory time argument by Little](../sources/Manufacturing-foundations-and-the-inventory-time-argument-by-Little.lingua#r_0088F3DFCA12E5E80C6DD9E45D) supplies the published area argument behind L=λW and emphasizes end effects and limiting conditions. Reconstruct that bounded argument using the original clipped intervals above and a family of fully completed traces. For a sufficient long-run result, assume D(T)/T→λ, W_D(T)→W, both finite, and R(T)/T→0. Taking limits in the exact equation gives time-average WIP L=λW. A fixed finite initial-history correction also vanishes after division by T. This is a sufficient sample-path statement, not every version of the queueing theorem.

If λ denotes arrival rate instead, also establish that arrival and departure rates have the same limit. The item balance gives their difference as inventory change divided by T; sublinear net inventory growth is sufficient. A growing backlog can violate that condition. Existence of a mean, stationarity, stability and a finite observation window are different premises. Do not use an average relationship to infer a maximum delay or a guarantee that every job will finish before a deadline.

In the four-slot optimal schedule, the two released jobs both finish, with residence times four and two. Area six divided by four gives average WIP 3/2; departure rate 2/4 times mean residence three gives the same result. The busier schedule includes unfinished jobs, so using only its one completed item's residence misses their accumulated time.

**Work:** compute the area independently by splitting time at every entry/exit and summing constant-count rectangles. Then compute clipped item durations and reconcile them. Extend the window in both directions and identify each changed boundary term. Supply a growing-backlog example and state why the selected limiting premises fail. Distinguish the true identity from evidence that a real measurement log recorded every entry, exit and item identity correctly.

